export const frontmatter = {
  title: 'The Activation-Function Ranking Depends On Your Block, Not Your Data',
  pubDate: '2026-07-03',
};
---

<BlogPostLayout frontmatter={frontmatter}>
  <p class="mb-6 text-gray-700">I set out to run a small benchmark to pick an activation function for a tiny transformer I was building — expecting to confirm the folk knowledge everyone teaches: tanh saturates, ReLU dies, GELU is the modern default, SiLU is a slightly better ReLU, and Mish is a slightly better SiLU. Two hundred and seventy-two runs later, that story has holes in it. This post walks through what I actually saw across seven activation functions and five tasks, and focuses on the finding that changed how I think about the choice: <b>which activation wins doesn't depend on your data, it depends on your block.</b></p>

  <div class="mb-6 p-4 rounded border bg-blue-50 text-blue-900">
    <b>TL;DR.</b> <b>SwiGLU wins every transformer task tested</b> — <span class="tt" tabindex="0" data-tip="WikiText-2: ~2M tokens of verified-good Wikipedia articles. Small and clean, so a model this size overfits it within a few epochs — which makes activation differences easy to see.">WikiText-2</span> LM by 11.6 PPL, <span class="tt" tabindex="0" data-tip="WikiText-103: ~100M tokens (≈50× WikiText-2) from the same Wikipedia source. Tests whether the small-data ranking survives at larger scale.">WikiText-103</span> by 1.55 PPL, <span class="tt" tabindex="0" data-tip="CIFAR-10: 60,000 32×32 color images in 10 classes (50K train / 10K test). The canonical small vision benchmark.">CIFAR-10</span> ViT by +2.5% top-1 (all with <span class="tt" tabindex="0" data-tip="Cohen's d: a standardized effect size — mean difference divided by the pooled standard deviation. d≈0.8 is already 'large'; d>2 means the two groups barely overlap, an unusually big and unambiguous gap.">Cohen's <i>d</i> ≥ 2</span>, most with <i>p</i> &lt; 10<sup>-4</sup>). <b>GELU is universal #2.</b> But two textbook claims fail. First, "tanh saturates" doesn't hold at this scale — only 0.4% of post-activations saturate at the end of training, and the deep-layer gradient is no smaller than GELU's, so whatever hurts tanh here, it isn't the vanishing-gradient story. Second, on the same CIFAR-10 dataset a ResNet+BN prefers SiLU (85.7% top-1) while a ViT-tiny prefers SwiGLU/GELU (77.1%). The block, not the modality, drives the ranking — and a gradient-flow probe that predicts rank on transformers flips sign under BatchNorm.
  </div>

