  <div class="mt-10 flex items-center gap-3">
    <a href="https://github.com/eitamar-saraf/act-bench" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-5 py-3 rounded border text-blue-700 hover:bg-blue-50 text-base md:text-lg">Repository</a>
  </div>

  <p class="mt-6 text-base md:text-lg text-gray-600">Caveats to keep me honest: every model here is small (19M parameters for the biggest, under 300K for ResNet-20), and every dataset fits comfortably on a workstation. All effects are measured with N=8 seeds per (activation, task) — enough to run Welch's <i>t</i>-tests but not enough to make a definitive claim about tiny gaps like SiLU vs Mish. Every training run is in fp32; results with mixed precision would smear the activation-tail statistics this post relies on. And no run is longer than a single epoch schedule — I did not test whether the ranking survives 10× training compute at the same model size. The seven-activation × five-task × eight-seed grid, the analysis scripts, and the raw logs are all in the <a href="https://github.com/eitamar-saraf/act-bench" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">repository</a>.</p>

  <p class="mt-4 text-base md:text-lg text-gray-600">Related: <a href="/blog/batchnorm" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">why BatchNorm helps</a> and <a href="/blog/batchnorm-poisoning" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">when it hurts</a> — the same layer that changes activation rankings has more than one way to bite.</p>

